<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sabratha</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sabratha was an ancient city on the coast of western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/libya">Libya</a>, founded as a Phoenician trading post and later one of the three Emporia that gave Tripolitania its name. It prospered under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> from the export of goods brought by Saharan caravans, and its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a> of the late 2nd century CE is one of the best preserved in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a>. The site is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sabratha.jpg" alt="Sabratha" width="320" height="171" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Theatre_of_Sabratha,_Libya.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
